# Unslop

Make the requested material clearer and more deliberate while preserving its meaning, behavior, and voice. Apply plain writing to replies without turning each reply into a review exercise.

## Scope

Use the supplied text, files, page, or diff. If no target is supplied, use the current task's changes. Resolve the review base from the repository when a diff comparison is needed. Review all requested routes when the user asks for the whole site.

Read only the relevant references:

| Material | Reference |
|---|---|
| Copy or substantive prose editing | [Copy](references/copy.md) |
| Rendered interface | [UI](references/ui.md) |
| Source code | [Code](references/code.md) |
| Skills and agent instructions | [Skill voice](references/skill-voice.md) |

## Editing standard

Cut repetition, unsupported claims, stock phrasing, and elements without a useful purpose. Keep concrete language, natural rhythm, and the requested tone. Avoid em dashes. Preserve technical terminology when it is the precise word.

Follow the shared [design preferences](../../AGENTS.md#visual-and-design-work). A card, icon, control, or abstraction may stay when it does real work. Do not remove accessibility, validation, or security behavior for visual or stylistic uniformity.

Check the changed material once and fix remaining concrete defects. Repeat verification when an edit creates a new risk; do not require counts or fixed rounds for their own sake. For an explicit instruction rewrite, remove obsolete process rules as well as rephrasing them.

Report the meaningful changes and any material limitation. For a routine final pass, include only what helps the main task's delivery; a separate slop report is unnecessary.

